<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rhine Province</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1822">1822</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Rhenish Prussia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> Province was a province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, formed in 1822 from lands on both banks of the Rhine that Prussia gained at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> as its capital. It absorbed many former ecclesiastical states, imperial cities and small principalities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> became its largest city. It was divided after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rhine-province.jpg" alt="Rhine Province" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Preu%C3%9Fen_-_Rheinland.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Rhine Province, also called Rhenish Prussia, was the westernmost province of the Kingdom of Prussia and later of the Free State of Prussia, from 1822 to 1946. Its capital was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, and the provincial assembly met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dusseldorf">Düsseldorf</a>. Cologne was the largest and most important city. The province was divided into five districts, Koblenz, Düsseldorf, Cologne, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a>, and about 200 km of the Rhine lay inside it. In 1905 it had 6,435,778 inhabitants, mostly Catholics on the left bank, and it was the most densely populated part of Prussia. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a>, iron and other minerals made it the most important manufacturing district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, with Aachen and Düsseldorf as its two chief industrial centres.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> gained the duchies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, Berg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a> and Jülich, the ecclesiastical principalities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a> and Cologne and nearly a hundred small lordships and abbeys. In 1822 these were joined to form the Rhine Province, which had 8.0 million inhabitants by 1939. After the Armistice of 1918 Allied forces occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> continued the occupation and demilitarised the area. The treaty separated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saar-river">Saar</a> from the province and gave the cantons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eupen">Eupen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malmedy">Malmedy</a> and Sankt Vith to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. The last Allied troops left in 1930.</p><p>On 7 March 1936 Nazi Germany remilitarised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> with very little force, in violation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>. France did not intervene, and the British government did not oppose the step in principle, though it disliked the manner of it. In the fighting of 1944 and 1945 the U.S. Army lost 24,000 troops in the Hürtgen Forest. In February and March 1945 Operation Veritable cleared German forces from the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and on 7 March 1945 U.S. troops captured the last intact Rhine bridge at Remagen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s unconditional surrender in 1945 the province was split between the French and British occupation zones, and it was abolished in August 1946. Its northern part, under British administration, merged with the former province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-rhine-westphalia">North Rhine-Westphalia</a>, and most of the southern part, under French administration, joined the Palatinate and other territories to form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a> became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in February 1946 and joined the Federal Republic in 1957. The Landschaftsverband Rheinland, set up in 1953 within North Rhine-Westphalia, can be regarded as the direct successor of the province's administration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rhine Province” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Prussia's survival was not assured: the Centre Party's council in the <b>Rhine Province</b> called for a Rhineland-Westphalian republic, 600,000 signed for autonomy in Hanover, and a revolt in Posen aimed at restoring Poland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-state-of-prussia">Free State of Prussia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Wied-Neuwied was mediatised in 1806 and placed under the Duchy of Nassau, then in 1815 under Prussia as part of the <b>Rhine Province</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wied-neuwied">County of Wied-Neuwied</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1815 it passed to Prussia and became the main city of the <b>Rhine Province</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Free Imperial City of Cologne</a></li><li><blockquote>It was placed under provisional administration, and in 1815 the Congress of Vienna gave it to Prussia as part of the <b>Rhine Province</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/grand-duchy-of-berg">Grand Duchy of Berg</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Greater Hamburg Act of 1937, Oldenburg ceded Birkenfeld to Prussia, which made it a district of the <b>Rhine Province</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-birkenfeld">Principality of Birkenfeld</a></li><li><blockquote>It became the district of St. Wendel in the <b>Rhine Province</b> and is now part of Saarland.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-lichtenberg">Principality of Lichtenberg</a></li></ul></section>
